{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Avram journeyed on, journeying steadily to the Negev.</b> Perhaps he wanted to grow wise in matters of divinity, for whoever wants to grow wise should face south (Bava Batra 25a). But wealth he did not seek, for he said: ״אִם אֶקַּח מִחוּט וְעַד שְׂרוֹךְ נַעַל״ (Genesis 14:23). And as for those gifts that he received on Sarah's account — he was compelled to accept them so that they would not kill him, for they all gave to him by way of flattery, perhaps hoping to entice him to give them his sister as a wife. And had he not accepted, they would have thought that he did not want to give her to them and they would have killed him. Nevertheless, he did not wish to benefit from those gifts and gave them to the sons of the concubines, as it says (Genesis 25:6): ״וְלִבְנֵי הַפִּלַגְשִׁים נָתַן אַבְרָהָם מַתָּנוֹת״. That is, those gifts that he had already received from the Egyptians, he gave to the sons of the concubines — meaning the sons of Keturah, and she is Hagar the Egyptian, as Rashi explains. For from the Egyptians he had taken the gifts and returned them to their own stock, giving them to the sons of Hagar the Egyptian."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:07.310614736Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}